.\" The man page of glaze. cmd/glaze/man_test.go checks that it names every command and every flag.
.TH GLAZE 1 "2026-11-06" "Glazier" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
glaze \- manage tmux sessions, windows and panes from a profile
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B glaze
.RI [ global\ options ]
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B glaze up
.RB [ \-\-detached ]
.RB [ \-\-clear ]
.RB [ \-\-profile\-path
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-\-var
.IR key=value ]
.br
.B glaze down
.RB [ \-\-session
.IR name ]
.br
.B glaze ls
.br
.B glaze format
.RB [ \-\-validate ]
.RB [ \-\-stdout ]
.br
.B glaze save
.RB [ \-\-session
.IR name ]
.RB [ \-\-stdout ]
.br
.B glaze completion
.IR shell
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B glaze
creates a tmux session with its windows and panes from a profile. A profile is a
.B .glaze
file in the HCL language. It declares the session, each window, each pane, the
commands that run in a pane and the tmux options and hooks.
.B glaze
validates the profile before it starts tmux, so an error in the profile stops
the command before a session exists.
.PP
A profile runs its commands and hooks in your shell, with your permissions.
Read a profile from another person before you apply it.
.B glaze format \-\-validate
checks the syntax, not what the commands do.
.PP
.B glaze
needs tmux 3.2a or later on
.BR PATH .
.B up
and
.B save
check the version first and stop with exit status 4 on an older tmux.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS up
Apply a profile. The command creates the session, the windows and the panes,
runs the commands of each pane and attaches your terminal to the session. When
a session with the same name runs, the command attaches to it and changes
nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-detached
Create the session and do not attach to it.
.TP
.B \-\-clear
First kill an existing session that has the same name.
.B glaze
refuses when it runs inside that session, because the kill would also end
.BR glaze .
.TP
.B \-\-keep\-on\-failure
Keep the partly built session when
.B up
fails, so that you can examine it. Without the flag,
.B glaze
removes the session that this run created.
.TP
.B \-\-debug
Print each command that
.B glaze
sends to the tmux socket, and the text of each pane and session command. Env
values show as
.BR <redacted> .
.TP
.BI \-\-command\-timeout " duration"
Stop the wait for the commands of a pane after this duration, for example
.BR 5m .
The default value
.B 0
waits with no limit.
.TP
.BI \-\-profile\-path " path"
The path to a
.B .glaze
file. See
.BR PROFILES .
.TP
.BI \-\-socket\-path " path"
The path to a custom tmux socket.
.TP
.BI \-\-socket\-name " name"
The name of a custom tmux socket.
.TP
.BI \-\-var " key=value"
Set a variable. The flag is repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-var\-file " path"
An HCL file of variable values.
.SS down
Kill the session that a profile describes. The command evaluates only the
session name of the profile. A session that does not run causes no error.
.TP
.BI \-\-session " name"
Kill the session with this name. No profile is required.
.TP
.BI \-\-profile\-path " path"
.TQ
.BI \-\-socket\-path " path"
.TQ
.BI \-\-socket\-name " name"
.TQ
.BI \-\-var " key=value"
.TQ
.BI \-\-var\-file " path"
As for
.BR up .
.SS ls
List the sessions that run on the tmux server: the name, the number of
windows, the path and a marker for an attached session. The command succeeds
with an empty table when no server runs.
.TP
.BI \-\-socket\-path " path"
.TQ
.BI \-\-socket\-name " name"
As for
.BR up .
.SS format
Rewrite a profile in the canonical format. The command writes the file through
a temporary file and one rename, and leaves a file that is already formatted
as it is.
.TP
.B \-\-stdout
Print the formatted profile and do not write the file.
.TP
.B \-\-validate
Check the profile and print each diagnostic. Every declared variable must get
a value from
.B \-\-var
or from a default.
.TP
.BI \-\-profile\-path " path"
.TQ
.BI \-\-var " key=value"
.TQ
.BI \-\-var\-file " path"
As for
.BR up .
.SS save
Write the structure of a running session as a profile: the names of the
session, the windows and the panes, their directories, the active window and
pane, and the layout of each window. The command does not save commands,
environment variables, hooks or options, because tmux reports the whole state
and not what a profile set, and a saved command would run again on each
.BR up .
.TP
.BI \-\-profile\-path " path"
The path of the profile to write. The default is
.B .glaze
in the current directory. The command refuses an existing file.
.TP
.BI \-\-session " name"
Save the session with this name. The default is the session that
.B glaze
runs in.
.TP
.B \-\-stdout
Print the profile and do not write a file.
.TP
.B \-\-force
Replace the file at
.B \-\-profile\-path
when it exists.
.TP
.BI \-\-socket\-path " path"
.TQ
.BI \-\-socket\-name " name"
As for
.BR up .
.SS completion
Print a completion script for
.BR bash ,
.BR zsh ,
.B fish
or
.BR pwsh .
The script asks
.B glaze
for the commands and the flags on each Tab. The packages install the scripts.
With a release zip or
.BR "go install" ,
load the script yourself:
.PP
.RS
.nf
source <(glaze completion bash)        # add to ~/.bashrc
source <(glaze completion zsh)         # add to ~/.zshrc
glaze completion fish > ~/.config/fish/completions/glaze.fish
.fi
.RE
.SS help
Show the help of
.B glaze
or of one command.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
Put a global option before the command.
.TP
.BI \-\-log\-level " level"
One of
.BR trace ,
.BR debug ,
.BR info ,
.BR warning ,
.B error
or
.BR critical .
The default is
.BR info .
.TP
.BR \-\-version ", " \-v
Print the version, the stage, the commit and the build date.
.B glaze up \-v
is a usage error.
.TP
.BR \-\-help ", " \-h
Show the help.
.SH PROFILES
.B glaze
looks for a profile in this order: the
.B \-\-profile\-path
value,
.B .glaze
in the current directory, then
.B .glaze
in the directory that
.B GLAZE_PATH
names. A
.B ~
or
.B ~/
at the start of a path expands to your home directory.
.PP
A minimal profile:
.PP
.RS
.nf
session {
  name              = "api"
  starting_directory = "~/src/api"

  window {
    name   = "editor"
    layout = "main-vertical"

    pane {
      name     = "edit"
      commands = ["nvim ."]
    }

    pane {
      name     = "shell"
    }
  }
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
The README of Glazier describes the full format: variables, locals, string
functions, sizes, adjustments, options, hooks and the session environment.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B GLAZE_PATH
The directory that holds the
.B .glaze
file that
.B glaze
uses when the current directory has none.
.TP
.BI GLAZE_ENV_ NAME
The value of
.BI env. NAME
in a profile.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
When set,
.B glaze
writes no colour.
.B glaze
writes colour only when standard error is a terminal.
.TP
.B TMUX
Set by tmux in a pane.
.B glaze
uses it to find the server and the session that it runs in, for
.B save
and for the attach of
.BR up .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.B ls
with no server and
.B down
for a session that does not run also succeed.
.TP
.B 1
A run failed, for example because tmux rejected an option value or
.B glaze
could not write a profile.
.TP
.B 2
The command line is not correct, for example an unknown flag or a
.B \-\-var
without
.BR = .
.TP
.B 3
The profile has errors, or
.B glaze
cannot find the profile.
.TP
.B 4
.B glaze
cannot use tmux, for example because tmux is not on
.BR PATH ,
is older than 3.2a or the socket does not give access.
.TP
.B 130
SIGINT stopped
.BR glaze .
.TP
.B 143
SIGTERM stopped
.BR glaze .
.PP
When
.B up
fails or a signal stops it,
.B glaze
removes the session that this run created.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B .glaze
The profile in the current directory.
.TP
.BI $GLAZE_PATH /.glaze
The profile that
.B glaze
uses when the current directory has none.
.SH EXAMPLES
Apply the profile in the current directory and attach:
.PP
.RS
.nf
glaze up
.fi
.RE
.PP
Build a session in the background with a variable:
.PP
.RS
.nf
glaze up \-\-detached \-\-var district=watson
.fi
.RE
.PP
Check a profile without tmux:
.PP
.RS
.nf
glaze format \-\-validate \-\-profile\-path gig.glaze
.fi
.RE
.PP
Save the session that you are in and print it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
glaze save \-\-stdout > saved.glaze
.fi
.RE
.PP
Kill a session by name:
.PP
.RS
.nf
glaze down \-\-session api
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tmux (1)
.PP
The README and the specification: https://github.com/wilhelm-murdoch/glazier
